Defense-in-depth: a buffer map() must not report success with invalid planes.

A QAbstractVideoBuffer::map() implementation that returns planeCount > 0 with
null plane pointers (e.g. the retained-blank-m_swFrame state fixed by patch
0001) currently propagates as a *successful* QVideoFrame::map(); the CPU
converter then dereferences the null planes. Validate every reported plane at
the narrowest safe layer and fail the map instead.

--- a/src/multimedia/video/qvideoframe.cpp
+++ b/src/multimedia/video/qvideoframe.cpp
@@ -xx,6 +xx,17 @@ bool QVideoFrame::map(QVideoFrame::MapMode mode)
     d->mapData = d->videoBuffer->map(mode);
     if (d->mapData.planeCount == 0)
         return false;
 
+    // Reject a "successful" map whose required planes are unusable: a null
+    // data pointer, a negative stride, or a negative size on any reported
+    // plane means the underlying buffer failed without saying so. Treating
+    // it as success hands invalid planes to the pixel converters.
+    for (int i = 0; i < d->mapData.planeCount; ++i) {
+        if (d->mapData.data[i] == nullptr || d->mapData.bytesPerLine[i] < 0
+            || d->mapData.dataSize[i] < 0) {
+            qWarning("QVideoFrame::map: buffer reported %d planes but plane %d is invalid",
+                     d->mapData.planeCount, i);
+            d->mapData = {};
+            return false;
+        }
+    }
+
     d->mapMode = mode;
